xrdp performance notes (Ubuntu 24.04, KDE Plasma X11, no GPU)
Last updated: 2026-09-23 (lean pass added)

Client: Windows mstsc, 1280x800 session (per xrdp.log). A smaller window means fewer pixels to encode per frame.

What made it smooth
===================

1. KDE compositing off
   Without a GPU, KWin renders effects in software (llvmpipe). That burns CPU and sends extra redraws over RDP.
     kwriteconfig5 --file kwinrc --group Compositing --key Enabled false   # kwriteconfig6 on Plasma 6
     qdbus org.kde.KWin /KWin reconfigure
   Quick toggle for testing: Alt+Shift+F12.

2. KDE animations off (animation speed "Instant")
     ~/.config/kwinrc      [Compositing] AnimationSpeed=0
     ~/.config/kdeglobals  AnimationDurationFactor=0
   Also helps: solid-colour wallpaper, no blur/translucency effects.

3. xrdp upgraded from 0.9.24 (distro) to 0.10.6.1, xorgxrdp from 0.9.19 to 0.10.5
   Built from source in /usr/local/src/xrdp-build (configure: --enable-x264 --enable-pixman
   --enable-jpeg --enable-fuse --enable-opus --enable-mp3lame). Distro packages removed.
   0.10 adds the GFX pipeline. Confirmed in the log on 2026-09-22: the session uses
   "gfx rfx pro" (RemoteFX Progressive over GFX), not H.264. The server offers H264 first,
   but the client matched RFX. This is likely the main win over 0.9.
   Check the negotiated codec:
     sudo grep -aiE 'gfx|rfx|h\.?264|avc|codec' /var/log/xrdp.log | tail -20
   Look for "Matched RFX mode" and "starting gfx rfx pro codec session".
   GFX needs max_bpp=32 (xrdp refuses GFX at 24), so keep max_bpp=32.

4. Bigger TCP buffers
   /etc/xrdp/xrdp.ini:
     tcp_send_buffer_bytes=4194304
     tcp_recv_buffer_bytes=4194304
   /etc/sysctl.d/90-xrdp.conf (raises the kernel limit so the buffer sizes take effect):
     net.core.wmem_max=8388608
     net.core.rmem_max=8388608

Required fix after the upgrade (not a speed change)
===================================================
The 0.10 default sesman.ini starts the "Xorg" wrapper, which Xwrapper.config
(allowed_users=console) refuses for RDP sessions. /etc/xrdp/sesman.ini now uses:
  param=/usr/lib/xorg/Xorg

Other differences from the 0.9 config come from the 0.10 defaults, not from tuning:
frame intervals (h264/rfx/normal), login screen colours, the removed [vnc-any] section.

Backups and rollback
====================
  ~/xrdp.ini.bak                       original 0.9 xrdp.ini
  /root/xrdp-etc-backup-<timestamp>    full /etc/xrdp before the upgrade
Rollback: remove /etc/xrdp, restore the backup to /etc/xrdp, then
  sudo apt install xrdp xorgxrdp
Restarting xrdp also restarts xrdp-sesman (BindsTo=xrdp.service). Running X sessions become
orphaned: the new sesman does not know them, so a reconnect starts a second Plasma session for
the same user, which hangs on a black screen. Log out of RDP before any xrdp restart.
Cleanup if it happens: kill the orphaned Xorg (pgrep -a Xorg), then reconnect.

Setup script
============
~/xrdp-tune/xrdp-setup.sh installs everything on a fresh Ubuntu 24.04 server (run with sudo):
KDE Plasma (X11) + apps, xrdp v0.10.6.1 + xorgxrdp v0.10.5 built from source, the xrdp.ini,
gfx.toml (H.264 bitrate cap)
and sesman.ini changes, startwm.sh, /etc/sysctl.d/90-xrdp.conf, ~/.xsession, and the KDE
compositing/animation settings for the desktop user.
  sudo ./xrdp-setup.sh             # desktop user = the user who ran sudo
  sudo ./xrdp-setup.sh someuser
If the distro xrdp is installed, the script backs up /etc/xrdp and removes the packages first.

Snappiness tuning for the Android Windows App client (2026-09-22)
================================================================
Goal: lowest input latency at 1280x800. Colour depth stays at 32 bpp: below 32 xrdp turns
off GFX and falls back to the older, slower path.

Server (~/xrdp-tune/xrdp-snappy.sh, run with sudo):
  /etc/sysctl.d/91-xrdp-latency.conf
    net.ipv4.tcp_notsent_lowat=16384        # no stale frames queued behind the 4 MB send buffer
    net.ipv4.tcp_slow_start_after_idle=0    # fast first redraw after a pause
    net.ipv4.tcp_congestion_control=bbr     # better on lossy mobile links
  /etc/modules-load.d/tcp_bbr.conf          # loads tcp_bbr at boot
  /etc/xrdp/xrdp.ini [Xorg] rfx_frame_interval=16   # was 32; about 60 fps cap instead of 30
  Backup: /etc/xrdp/xrdp.ini.pre-snappy

Desktop (fewer screen changes, so less to encode and send):
  kdeglobals [KDE] CursorBlinkRate=0         # no cursor blink in Qt/KDE apps
  Konsole profile "Snappy" (default): BlinkingCursorEnabled=false, BlinkingTextEnabled=false
  VS Code: cursorBlinking solid, smooth scrolling off, terminal cursor blink off
  Chrome: chrome://flags/#smooth-scrolling = Disabled (set by hand)
  Baloo file indexer disabled (balooctl disable)
  Fonts already had antialiasing off (XftAntialias=false), which compresses best.

Client (Windows App on Android):
  Display: custom resolution 1280x800, scale 100%.
  Prefer 5 GHz Wi-Fi over mobile data.
  After connecting, check which codec the client chose (H.264 or RFX) with the log grep above.
  H.264 uses less bandwidth on weak links; RFX is sharper on fast ones. Force the order in
  /etc/xrdp/gfx.toml [codec] order = [...] if needed.

Bandwidth cap for slow links (2026-09-22)
=========================================
Goal: force a low-quality image so the Windows App client on mobile data stays responsive.
The whole framebuffer is encoded, so every application in the session (and RemoteApp) is
capped the same way. There is no per-user or per-session override in stock xrdp.

/etc/xrdp/gfx.toml [x264.default]
  vbv_max_bitrate = 1_500     # kbps, was 0 (uncapped)
  vbv_buffer_size = 150       # kbit
The lan/wan/... sections inherit from default, so the cap applies to every connection type.
Backup: /etc/xrdp/gfx.toml.pre-cap

The cap only applies when the client negotiates H.264. Checked on 2026-09-22 18:40: the
Windows App (Android) client matches RFX ("Codec search order is H264, RFX" then "Matched
RFX mode"), so it has no H.264 decode and the cap is idle for it. Do NOT force
order = [ "H.264" ]: a client without H.264 would then get no GFX match and fall back to
the old bitmap path. The cap stays in place for clients that do support H.264: with the
order left as H264, RFX each client gets the best it can decode. Windows mstsc picks H.264
when it has hardware decode (or GPO "Prefer AVC hardware encoding" / "Configure H.264/AVC
444 graphics mode" is on); check per client with the log grep above.
Do not lower max_bpp or disable GFX either, same fallback.

RFX progressive has no quality or bitrate knob. Levers that do work for this client:
  - Smaller client resolution in Windows App (e.g. 1024x640). Biggest win.
  - /etc/xrdp/xrdp.ini [Xorg] rfx_frame_interval=32 (30 fps instead of 60). Halves bandwidth
    on busy screens, costs snappiness.
Restart xrdp after editing (log out of RDP first, see the orphaned-session warning above).

Lean pass: last-bit latency tuning (2026-09-23)
==============================================
Script: ~/xrdp-tune/xrdp-lean.sh (run with sudo). It never restarts xrdp. Kernel and KDE parts apply
at once / at next Plasma login; the xrdp parts wait for a manual restart:
  log out of RDP, then  sudo systemctl daemon-reload && sudo systemctl restart xrdp
Backups: /etc/xrdp/{xrdp.ini,sesman.ini,startwm.sh}.pre-lean

Already in place before this pass, so nothing more to gain there: GFX RFX Progressive
(client has no H.264), rfxcodec built with nasm SIMD, BBR + notsent_lowat, compositing off,
0% CPU steal on this VPS, client connects over the public interface (not tun0).

1. Channels: only cliprdr and drdynvc stay on. drdynvc carries GFX, so it must stay.
   rdpdr (client drive/printer redirection into the session, i.e. phone storage; nothing to
   do with Google Drive), rdpsnd (audio), rail (RemoteApp) and xrdpvr (video) are off.
   Less chansrv work, fewer channel round trips at connect. Re-enable in xrdp.ini [Channels].
2. Priority: systemd drop-ins  xrdp.service Nice=-10, xrdp-sesman.service Nice=-5.
   The encoder runs inside the xrdp connection process. sesman's children (Xorg, chansrv)
   inherit -5; startwm.sh does `renice -n 0 -p $$` so the desktop runs at 0 again.
   Effect: plasmashell/browser spikes no longer steal CPU from the encoder.
3. net.core.default_qdisc=fq (/etc/sysctl.d/92-xrdp-lean.conf) and `tc qdisc replace ... fq`
   live. BBR paces natively in fq. Small.
4. tls_ciphers prefers ECDHE AES-128-GCM (AES-NI). TLS 1.2 kept: the Android client may
   not do 1.3. Optional: ssl_protocols=TLSv1.3 saves a handshake round trip only.
5. LogLevel=WARNING in xrdp.ini and sesman.ini ([Logging] and [ChansrvLogging]).
   The codec grep in these notes needs INFO: set it back when checking the negotiated codec.
6. startwm.sh runs `xset s off -dpms`: no blanking, so no full-screen redraw after idle.
7. EXPERIMENTAL tcp_send_buffer_bytes=524288 (was 4194304). With tcp_notsent_lowat already
   in place the gain is unsure. If scrolling feels worse on a fast link, restore 4194304.
8. EXPERIMENTAL h264/rfx_frame_interval=8 (was 16): ~120 fps cap. Only matters on a 120 Hz
   phone; costs CPU during motion. Restore 16 if the encoder pegs a core (top, process xrdp).
11. KDE: remaining KWin effects off (dialogparent, dimscreen, maximize, morphingpopups,
    login/logout, fullscreen, zoom, presentwindows, highlightwindow, kscreen), Breeze window
    shadows off (breezerc ShadowSize=ShadowNone), Breeze widget animations off, tooltips off,
    launch feedback (bouncing cursor, taskbar button) off. Applies at next login.

Client (Windows App on Android), set by hand:
  - Redirection: sound, storage, camera, microphone off. Clipboard on.
  - Mouse mode "Mouse pointer" rather than touch: fewer events per gesture.
  - Phone: 5 GHz Wi-Fi, high refresh rate on, battery saver off during the session.

Verify after restart:
  ps -o pid,ni,comm -C xrdp,Xorg,xrdp-chansrv,plasmashell   # -10 / -5 / -5 / 0
  tc qdisc show dev eth0 | head -1                            # fq

Ugly pass: looks traded for latency (2026-09-23)
================================================
Script: ~/xrdp-tune/xrdp-ugly.sh (sudo, no restart). Backup /etc/xrdp/xrdp.ini.pre-ugly.
Colour depth is not touched: below 32 bpp GFX turns off, which is slower.
  - new_cursors=false: legacy 16-colour cursor shapes, smaller updates. Needs xrdp restart.
  - Breeze Dark flat theme, Fusion widget style, no window border, no shadows, no title
    gradient, solid-colour wallpaper. RFX compresses flat regions far better than gradients.
  - Fonts: antialias off, hintfull, no subpixel, also via ~/.config/fontconfig/fonts.conf
    so GTK/Chrome follow. 1-bit glyphs compress best.
  - Cursor breeze_cursors 24 px. Panel clock without seconds. Notifications: Do Not Disturb
    until 9999, sounds off. Each popup or tick is a frame.
Manual: client resolution 1024x640 (biggest win), Chrome reduce motion + force dark,
VS Code minimap off. Rollback in the script's last lines.
